Jenkins is an automation tool mainly used for CI/CD (Continuous Integration / Continuous Delivery).
In simple words:   Jenkins automatically takes your code → builds it → tests it → creates a Docker image → deploys it to Kubernetes.
"Jenkins is an open-source automation server used to automate CI/CD processes such as building, testing, and deploying applications."


Example
Suppose you have a developer:

Developer
   |
   | git push
   ↓
GitHub
   |
   ↓
Jenkins
   |
   ├── 1. Get latest code
   ├── 2. Build application
   ├── 3. Run tests
   ├── 4. Build Docker image
   ├── 5. Push image to Docker Hub/ECR
   └── 6. Deploy to Kubernetes
             |
             ↓
          EKS Cluster
             |
             ↓
           Pods
Why do we use Jenkins?

Without Jenkins, you might manually do:
git pull
mvn package
docker build
docker push
kubectl apply

Every time the developer changes code.

With Jenkins, you can automate these steps:
Developer pushes code
        ↓
Jenkins automatically starts
        ↓
Build
        ↓
Test
        ↓
Docker image
        ↓
Push to registry
        ↓
Deploy to EKS
